  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nTonight:\nQuiet weather tonight with cloud cover beginning to increase \novernight. Patchy fog is possible across southern Minnesota \nwhere 1-2: of rain fell last night, most pronounced in valleys &\nother low-lying areas.\n\nThursday Morning:\nA low-level jet over the central plains strengthens overnight,\nnosing into southern Minnesota & southwest Wisconsin by early\ntomorrow morning. Scattered showers & thunderstorms are expected\nto develop on the nose of this jet, with the best chance for\nsome rain expected from southern Minnesota into western\nWisconsin. Sporadic wind gusts of 30-40 mph & small hail can't\nbe ruled out with the strongest of these thunderstorms, but the\nthreat for any strong thunderstorms is higher off to our south.\nHigh-resolution models still remain widely varied in depicting \nhow theses showers & storms will evolve into tomorrow morning, \nso confidence remains low on forecasting just how widespread the\nrain will be & how long it may linger into Thursday morning.\n\nThursday afternoon/evening:\nThe answer to the above question will play a large role on what\nkind of potential may develop for thunderstorms later Thursday\nafternoon & evening. Synoptically, an impressive environment for\nsevere weather will develop over the upper Midwest Thursday as\nan upper level jet with 50 kts of deep shear develops long the\nnorthern tier of the CONUS, while an elevated mixed layer\nadvecting from over the Rockies provides very steep lapse rates\naround 8 C/km. However we won't have much of surface boundary\nover the area to initiate storms, so our severe potential will\ndepend greatly on how much instability we can realize by the\nafternoon. Warm & moist advection through the day will provide\nthe conditions for CAPE values to reach 3000-4000 J/kg - if\nwe're able to remain sunny through the afternoon & warm into the\nupper 80s to low 90s. It's no surprise that the same high-\nresolution modes that remain widely varied regarding the showers\n& thunderstorms tomorrow morning & also widely varied with how\nthey depict the second round of thunderstorms during the\nafternoon/evening. Generally, the wetter/cloudier solutions \nhold off any thunderstorms until later in the night when a \npotential MCS could arrive out of the Dakotas, while the drier \nsolutions are able to develop some discrete storms over central \n& southern Minnesota by late afternoon.\n\nConfidence is low, but the general trend seems to be towards the\nshowers & cloud cover lingering longer into the morning &\nafternoon. This does not mean we're done with a severe threat\ntomorrow evening though, as there are signs that thunderstorms\ndeveloping closer tot he surface low over the dakotas could \ncongeal into a MCS that eventually propagates through the area \nlater in the evening & night. Damaging wind will be the primary \nsevere threat with any storms that develop, especially if we \nget the forward-prooapgating MCS out of the Dakotas, while very \nlarge hail is possible earlier in the evening with any discrete \nstorms that develop over central/southern Minnesota. Heavy \ndownpours are expected with thunderstorms but they should be \nmoving fast enough to mitigate any heavy rain/flooding concerns.\n\n\nFriday-This Weekend:\nWarm advection continues into the weekend with a return to more\nsummer like humidity values, along with temperatures in the\nupper 80s to low 90s. Excessive heat could become a concerns as\nHeat Index values approach 100 degrees, but we are still coming\noff of a very warm July-August so acclimation means we're\nprobably able to push our excessive heat thresholds a little\nhigher than normal. Friday looks to be dry but another chance\nfor isolated to scattered thunderstorms looks likely overnight\ninto early saturday morning. Models remain widely spread in\npossible precipitation events through the rest of the weekend, \nso even though our forecast shows 30-40% PoPs, it won't be a \nwashout of a weekend.\n\nNext Week:\nThe upper air pattern remains active as a number of \ndisturbances ride over the ridge centered over the region. \nConfidence is low on when & where the greatest precipitation \nchances will be, but there at least appears to be some minor \nrelief coming from our drought. temperatures will depend on the \ntiming of any precipitation systems, but will tend to be above- \nnormal earlier in the week with some cooling relief likely later\nin the week.",
